{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1350831-dynast-abolitionist","id":1350831,"title":"Dynast Abolitionist","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Exalted"],"posts":[{"date":"2019-11-25T08:43:33-08:00","id":1350831,"title":"Dynast Abolitionist","content":"So yeah, I kinda like this angle brought up in the other thread, and want to spin it off here! What sort of Dynast might go down the path of working to end slavery? Would that call to any particular profession, or be more a question of personal circumstances/belief? How would this affect how others view them?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4086-croakamancer","name":"Croakamancer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4086_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"490"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-11-25T08:53:51-08:00","id":1350833,"title":"","content":"I don't know so much about the Dynasty, but my Eclipse in my group's Nexus game is leveraging his massive Resources, Factor status in the Guild, and pull with the Council of Entities to introduce policies that give economic incentives to those who own fewer slaves. He's been using his Bureaucracy Charms to speed such endeavors and slow attempts by others to curtail them. The other Eclipse has been writing plays that are both entertaining and impactful, turning popular opinion against the practice of slavery.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>He/him","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/177-thecountalucard","name":"TheCountAlucard","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar177_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10857"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-11-25T08:56:00-08:00","id":1350834,"title":"","content":"Don't know, but we do know that abolitionist Dynasts are a thing because the Deliberative made a law saying that a slave owner may only free one slave per year specifically to prevent the spread of abolitionists.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/151-hark","name":"Hark","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar151_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"932"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-11-25T09:45:02-08:00","id":1350840,"title":"","content":"Well you might be an abolitionist for lots of reasons.<br /> <br /> 1. You hate house Cynis. They\u2019ve got the slave monopoly, if you can outlaw slavery that\u2019s a real hit to their power.<br /> <br /> 2. You grew attached to your slaves growing up. You may have been taken care of by slaves and taught by slaves. Remember slaves in the Realm don\u2019t just do backbreaking manual labor, your accountant might be a slave, they might even be given a monetary allowance, but the thing to never forget is that they\u2019re not free. As desperately as they may want to leave or do something else they can\u2019t, no matter how well they\u2019re treated. So you might even have a close confidant who\u2019s a slave, that might give you some sympathies.<br /> <br /> 3. Religious reasons. The perfected hierarchy states that that those less enlightened serve those more enlightened, slavery is an unnatural enforcement of this natural law. Mortals must CHOOSE to serve. They may be corrected, imprisoned, even killed, but to enforce their servitude constantly would by like teaching math by simply reciting to the child what numbers to write. They perform the correct action without understanding. <br /> <br /> 4. You were yourself a slave. Anyone can exalt as a DB and there\u2019s always a chance it happens to a young Realm slave. <br /> <br /> If you are an abolitionist more than a few monks will think you\u2019re trying to shake up the perfected hierarchy, most slave owners will not see a problem, and think you\u2019re accusing them of some moral failing in spite of how well they treat their slaves. They\u2019ll probably also argue that it\u2019s not fair to take their slaves away when it\u2019s only a few bad egg slave owners. On the other hand your fellow abolitionists are likely to give you some help. There might be some who work together with their yearly slave releases to free whole families at once. Or who use legal loopholes to prevent people becoming slaves in the first place. You might even have a kind of ironic tendency to buy as many slaves as you could just to ensure they didn\u2019t end up somewhere worse. <br /> <br /> If your movement gets big enough and you get to be prominent enough in it be prepared to fend off assassins, and protect those close to you. They\u2019ll probably go after your slaves too just to spite you.<br /> <br /> With the civil war brewing too there\u2019s lots of opportunity for an upset. That\u2019s when it happened in America anyway.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1121,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1121-drlovemonkey","user":"DrLoveMonkey","date":". 11-25-2019, 12:48 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":8}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1121-drlovemonkey","name":"DrLoveMonkey","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1121_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4736"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-11-25T09:55:04-08:00","id":1350841,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Croakamancer</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1350831-dynast-abolitionist#post1350831\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Would that call to any particular profession, or be more a question of personal circumstances/belief?</div> </div> </div> </div>I'd say that such upper class philanthropy is more of a personal thing, although it can originate with the right kind of person getting a bad exposure to one of the most horrific examples of it. I think that professional interest in ending slavery tends to be a middle class thing.<br /> <br /> That said, there could be those who see abolition as a way to hurt House Cynis.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Croakamancer</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">How would this affect how others view them?</div> </div> </div> </div>Personal friends and family might see them as endearingly to soft-hearted for their own good, while society at large probably varies from seeing someone between a harmless eccentric and dangerous subversive. Either way, probably often mocked.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-11-25T10:44:34-08:00","id":1350855,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>DrLoveMonkey</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1350831-dynast-abolitionist?p=1350840#post1350840\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">2. You grew attached to your slaves growing up.</div> </div> </div> </div>I was going to say something similar regarding where most abolitionist thinking would come about in the Dynasty. <br /> <br /> Pro-slavery mentalities require enforcement of dehumanization to justify the arguments that slavery is a &quot;good&quot; thing by ignoring any individual suffering and only taking a broad perspective.<br /> <br /> Abolitionist thinking generally comes from reinforcement of the individual humanity of slaves.<br /> <br /> Under those generalities, having a deep emotional bond with your slaves as a Dynast means you're more likely to view them as deserving better.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-11-25T12:48:30-08:00","id":1350888,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1350831-dynast-abolitionist?p=1350855#post1350855\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I was going to say something similar regarding where most abolitionist thinking would come about in the Dynasty. <br /> <br /> Pro-slavery mentalities require enforcement of dehumanization to justify the arguments that slavery is a &quot;good&quot; thing by ignoring any individual suffering and only taking a broad perspective.<br /> <br /> Abolitionist thinking generally comes from reinforcement of the individual humanity of slaves.<br /> <br /> Under those generalities, having a deep emotional bond with your slaves as a Dynast means you're more likely to view them as deserving better.</div> </div> </div> </div>Although it can also lead to taking their presence and service for granted.<br /> <br /> Still, permission for individual manumission might be something that provides an outlet for that, where a combination of allowing occasional freedom and otherwise not being overtly abusive lets a lot of Dynasts part themselves on the back for being the &quot;good&quot; slaveowners (who are also dealing with the &quot;good&quot; slaves, the ones that are educated and erudite) and give no other consideration for the large groups.<br /> <br /> That and the times when the slaves with more personal contact are broken to continuously put on the act of being content with their state.<br /> <br /> It's evident from history that plenty of people have had no trouble reconciling personal endearment with their house slaves with the continuing of the institution.<br /> <br /> (The rationalisations for slavery need to also be concerned with the intrinsic systemic harms, since the evils of slavery are not an aggregate of individual abuses)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":158,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","user":"Isator Levi","date":". 11-25-2019, 03:50 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-11-25T13:36:35-08:00","id":1350900,"title":"","content":"Yeah, I didn't mean to say that abolitionist inclinations were inherent in this situation, just the most likely source of it for a Dynast.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-11-25T14:24:10-08:00","id":1350914,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Croakamancer</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1350831-dynast-abolitionist#post1350831\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So yeah, I kinda like this angle brought up in the other thread, and want to spin it off here! What sort of Dynast might go down the path of working to end slavery? Would that call to any particular profession, or be more a question of personal circumstances/belief? How would this affect how others view them?</div> </div> </div> </div>How familiar are you with the story of &quot;Moe and the Big Exit&quot;?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/899114-the-official-exalted-forum-introduction-thread?p=899399#post899399\" target=\"_blank\">Hi, I'm JohnDoe244</a>. My posts represent my opinions, not facts.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/531-johndoe244","name":"JohnDoe244","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar531_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2958"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-11-25T19:07:50-08:00","id":1350945,"title":"","content":"Another aspect is the type of slavery in effect. In more recent centuries, Slavery is of course associated with the Transatlantic slave trade from Africa, where chattel slavery is all about production, usually of cash crops like cotton, sugar or rubber.<br /> <br /> In different parts of the world and at different times, slavery was different. A smart youth with no prospects could sell themself into slavery, with their family profiting by the sale, and the youth could potentially be given an education at their purchaser\u2019s expense. The life of an educated slave in China or Rome was preferable to that of a rural peasant.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24172-sticks","name":"Sticks","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24172_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"86"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-11-25T22:01:52-08:00","id":1350973,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Sticks</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1350831-dynast-abolitionist?p=1350945#post1350945\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Another aspect is the type of slavery in effect. In more recent centuries, Slavery is of course associated with the Transatlantic slave trade from Africa, where chattel slavery is all about production, usually of cash crops like cotton, sugar or rubber.<br /> <br /> In different parts of the world and at different times, slavery was different. A smart youth with no prospects could sell themself into slavery, with their family profiting by the sale, and the youth could potentially be given an education at their purchaser\u2019s expense. The life of an educated slave in China or Rome was preferable to that of a rural peasant.</div> </div> </div> </div>Every time, this happens every time...<br /> <br /> \u200bYes, <i>some </i>slaves didn't have it as shitty as <i>other </i>slaves. However, they were still humans that were property and therefore subject to fewer rights. They could be sold to a crueler master, subjected to awful, arbitrary punishments, and worse.<br /> <br /> \u200bEven if it wasn't always the worst possible version of itself imaginable, the practice of slavery historically was still monstrous.<br /> <br /> Okay, yeah, being a Roman or Chinese peasant probably sucked - but you know what else sucked? Your owner getting to pierce your ears to show that he owns you forever, because when the time for your freedom came up, you wanted to stay with your wife (who he still owned).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>He/him","edited":{"userid":177,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/177-thecountalucard","user":"TheCountAlucard","date":". 11-26-2019, 01:10 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/177-thecountalucard","name":"TheCountAlucard","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar177_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10857"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-11-25T22:47:00-08:00","id":1350978,"title":"","content":"@TheCountAlucard<br /> <br /> Of course I\u2019m not defending slavery, I\u2019m just saying that slavery as an institution does not come in a single form, that the experience of a slave is universal and uniform.<br /> <br /> Every time, this happens every time someone tries to bring a bit of nuance to a discussion and there you are trying to shut them down for speaking up.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24172-sticks","name":"Sticks","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24172_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"86"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-11-26T02:10:21-08:00","id":1350999,"title":"","content":"In a general conversation about slavery, I think discussing the varied ways slavery existed (and why) is important nuance.<br /> <br /> For this example, it's less so. Slavery in the Realm and Threshold is generally presented as chattel slavery. While it bares more resemblance to the chattel slavery practiced in some of the ancient and classical Greek city states than the Transatlantic African slave trade, that nuance really doesn't matter much to Exalted. Exalted isn't a setting where the dominant style of slavery has a social function outside of forcing people into different types of labor for life with a lose of any liberty or rights.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-11-26T02:34:44-08:00","id":1351000,"title":"","content":"Answering the original question: becoming an abolitionist in such society, besides decency, would more importantly take extreme intellectual effort.<br /> <br /> Before I start: I know that due to understandable reasons the discussion of slavery is very emotionally loaded for Americans. However, abolitionist movement in US took place in a very different context than in what I discuss below and than in the setting of Exalted. In mid-ninetieth century slavery was already abolished (at least formally) in many countries, so they were fighting over a model already known and tested.<br /> <br /> That said, let me start the proper part of my post with an example. Some people in ethics discuss what are our moral responsibilities towards wild-living animals. In general, it can be argued that between predators, parasites and bad wheather condition, their lives have typically very low quality, so maybe in virtue of reducing suffering we should intervene more in the ecosystems, for instance, putting predators that prey on animals with developed brains in captive.<br /> <br /> If you are still reading this post, I presume that the idea in the last paragraph strikes you as something totally exotic and probably you are instinctively deeply opposed to it, even if you are, say, opposed to eating animals. I am not saying that the view presented above is right. However, this is something that can imagineably become a standard view in next two hundred years with people discussing how come no one was fighting for better life of wild animals. The honest answer would be then: we never thought about it.<br /> <br /> Now, if we go to a society where slavery is an omnipresent institution, the idea of abolitionism may become virtually unimaginable. <br /> <br /> In classic Rome, a free man never works for another free man. When Caesar Augustus was establishing something like state's office, it was obvious that those high-rank officials were chosen from among his slaves - they were working for him after all (later, equites started to take over these positions - too much money and power).<br /> <br /> As far as I know, in the whole vast ethical literature of antiquity there is no single person to question the institution of slavery. This includes philosophers who themselves were former slaves. It just didn't appear to them that society can be organised that way, the same way it doesn't appear to most of us that we could intervene into ecosystem out of care for animals. Of course, if you were a decent person, you probably treated your slaves well, you agreed them to marry their chosen spouse, maybe you even liberated the most trusted of them - thus you were not even dehumanising these people. If you were a philopher, you might encourage others to treat their slaves well and argue that this is the right thing to do. You just never thought that there is something wrong with the institution as a whole.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":13416,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13416-lanic","user":"Lanic","date":". 11-26-2019, 05:40 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13416-lanic","name":"Lanic","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13416_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"194"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-11-26T02:35:07-08:00","id":1351001,"title":"","content":"Yeah, Realm slaves are foreigners used for petty labour or brutal manual labour (salt mining, sex, etc) rather than, generally, doctors or teachers.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STing Bronze Age Exalted","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","name":"The Wizard of Oz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2402_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-11-26T03:45:28-08:00","id":1351005,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Wizard of Oz</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1350831-dynast-abolitionist?p=1351001#post1351001\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Yeah, Realm slaves are foreigners used for petty labour or brutal manual labour (salt mining, sex, etc) rather than, generally, doctors or teachers.</div> </div> </div> </div>Err... Doctors, less so, but teachers? There's a lot of establishment for Dynasts having slave tutors - multiple aspect books, and I think WFHW and The Realm kept that, no?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4086-croakamancer","name":"Croakamancer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4086_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"490"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-11-26T04:11:55-08:00","id":1351009,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Croakamancer</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1350831-dynast-abolitionist?p=1351005#post1351005\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Err... Doctors, less so, but teachers? There's a lot of establishment for Dynasts having slave tutors - multiple aspect books, and I think WFHW and The Realm kept that, no?</div> </div> </div> </div>If you appoint nine people to stock shelves and one person to run a register, can it not be said that your employees are <i>generally</i> stockers and not cashiers? What about when it's forty-nine stockers and one cashier?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>He/him","edited":{"userid":177,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/177-thecountalucard","user":"TheCountAlucard","date":". 11-26-2019, 07:26 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/177-thecountalucard","name":"TheCountAlucard","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar177_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10857"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-11-26T05:39:34-08:00","id":1351019,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>TheCountAlucard</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1350831-dynast-abolitionist?p=1351009#post1351009\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If you appoint nine people to stock shelves and one person to run a register, can it not be said that your employees are <i>generally</i> stockers and not cashiers? What about when it's forty-nine stockers and one cashier?</div> </div> </div> </div>Yes, but the position of cashier is widespread. As is slave tutors for dynasts, that's standard until they hit primary school. Slave nannys are standard too.<br /> <br /> Widespread educated slavery on the isle is an important point here, mostly because it's likely to be at the forefront of abolitionist thinking in context. To repurpose your metaphor - you are unlikely to have much interaction with a stocker, but the fact that the store has a cashier is someone you talk to, someone you might strike up a friendly conversation with. Ditto educated household slaves - it's a lot easier to see them as people when they're not busy labouring in the sun all day.<br /> <br /> That doesn't make it 'good slavery', it just makes it more likely to lead to abolitionism. And it's worth noting that slavery in the Realm is generally kinda a weird mishmash historically of various traditions.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4086-croakamancer","name":"Croakamancer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4086_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"490"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-11-26T07:58:45-08:00","id":1351040,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>TheCountAlucard</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1350831-dynast-abolitionist?p=1351009#post1351009\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If you appoint nine people to stock shelves and one person to run a register, can it not be said that your employees are <i>generally</i> stockers and not cashiers? What about when it's forty-nine stockers and one cashier?</div> </div> </div> </div>Eh, it's a fair point, Croakamancer's right.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STing Bronze Age Exalted","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","name":"The Wizard of Oz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2402_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-11-26T08:13:05-08:00","id":1351041,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lanic</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Now, if we go to a society where slavery is an omnipresent institution, the idea of abolitionism may become virtually unimaginable.<br /> <br /> In classic Rome, a free man never works for another free man. When Caesar Augustus was establishing something like state's office, it was obvious that those high-rank officials were chosen from among his slaves - they were working for him after all (later, equites started to take over these positions - too much money and power).<br /> <br /> As far as I know, in the whole vast ethical literature of antiquity there is no single person to question the institution of slavery. This includes philosophers who themselves were former slaves. It just didn't appear to them that society can be organised that way, the same way it doesn't appear to most of us that we could intervene into ecosystem out of care for animals. Of course, if you were a decent person, you probably treated your slaves well, you agreed them to marry their chosen spouse, maybe you even liberated the most trusted of them - thus you were not even dehumanising these people. If you were a philopher, you might encourage others to treat their slaves well and argue that this is the right thing to do. You just never thought that there is something wrong with the institution as a whole.</div> </div> </div> </div>So, this is a good point. But tbh, in the Realm slavery isn't an omnipresent institution (whereas serfdom is). Percentage-wise, slavery is quite uncommon (whereas in the Roman Empire, I think it was about a third of the population). Only Dragonblood are allowed to rent slaves, only the Government is allowed to own them, and only one family in the entire continent (a big family, but still) is allowed to import them.<br /> If you live in Pangu, it may seem... well, not omnipresent, but common. Everywhere else, it's not.<br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STing Bronze Age Exalted","edited":{"userid":2402,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","user":"The Wizard of Oz","date":". 11-26-2019, 11:36 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","name":"The Wizard of Oz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2402_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-11-26T09:13:39-08:00","id":1351055,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Wizard of Oz</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1350831-dynast-abolitionist?p=1351041#post1351041\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Percentage-wise, slavery is quite uncommon (whereas in the Roman Empire, I think it was about a third of the population).</div> </div> </div> </div>The vast majority of Roman slaves were still owned by a small percentage of the population. Same with the American South.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Wizard of Oz</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Only Dragonblood are allowed to rent slaves, only the Government is allowed to own them</div> </div> </div> </div>That convolution is not present in this Edition; the Dragon Blooded own slaves.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Wizard of Oz</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">only one family in the entire continent (a big family, but still) is allowed to import them.</div> </div> </div> </div>House Cynis is managing a business enterprise. The Realm slave trade is not a mom and pop operation, it's like fricking... Ford or something. They'll have armies of patricians to manage most of the day-to-day administration while they collect their sizable percentage.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Wizard of Oz</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">If you live in Pangu, it may seem... well, not omnipresent, but common. Everywhere else, it's not.<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>Slavery will be common in Ragara mines.<br /> <br /> Slavery will be common in V'neef vineyards.<br /> <br /> Peleps probably makes use of them to harvest lumber.<br /> <br /> And just generally utilised in commercial enterprises of the Dynasty, which blanket the Blessed Isle, as well as maintenance for various public works.<br /> <br /> Slavery will be evident to anybody who lives in proximity to Dynast property, and it will be in disproportionately large numbers because the Dynasts are extremely rich.<br /> <br /> This isn't the case of one of those countries where the vast populations of slaves owned by a tiny number of rich people are labouring on sugar plantations thousands of miles overseas.<br /> <br /> Edit: It also matters to the original point being made because slavery is going to be omnipresent to the only people that have any say in whether or not it continues.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":158,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","user":"Isator Levi","date":". 11-26-2019, 12:33 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-11-26T10:42:31-08:00","id":1351068,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Croakamancer</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1350831-dynast-abolitionist?p=1351005#post1351005\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Err... Doctors, less so, but teachers? There's a lot of establishment for Dynasts having slave tutors - multiple aspect books, and I think WFHW and The Realm kept that, no?</div> </div> </div> </div>Aaaaackutallly:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Realm</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Almost every Dynast has access to a slave-physician who advises how to alleviate and reverse pains, aches, and persistent annoyances.</div> </div> </div> </div>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/899114-the-official-exalted-forum-introduction-thread?p=899399#post899399\" target=\"_blank\">Hi, I'm JohnDoe244</a>. My posts represent my opinions, not facts.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/531-johndoe244","name":"JohnDoe244","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar531_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2958"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-11-26T10:52:45-08:00","id":1351069,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1350831-dynast-abolitionist?p=1351055#post1351055\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">That convolution is not present in this Edition; the Dragon Blooded own slaves.</div> </div> </div> </div>Far more shockingly, citizens of the Realm may now also be enslaved.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/899114-the-official-exalted-forum-introduction-thread?p=899399#post899399\" target=\"_blank\">Hi, I'm JohnDoe244</a>. My posts represent my opinions, not facts.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/531-johndoe244","name":"JohnDoe244","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar531_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2958"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-11-26T10:58:38-08:00","id":1351073,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>TheCountAlucard</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1350831-dynast-abolitionist?p=1351009#post1351009\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If you appoint nine people to stock shelves and one person to run a register, can it not be said that your employees are <i>generally</i> stockers and not cashiers? What about when it's forty-nine stockers and one cashier?</div> </div> </div> </div>Even in Roman society, most of slaves were obviously relegated to menial, physical jobs. Still, this didn't make any known author, including former slaves, express their concern about this institution.<br /> <br /> People were opposed to making slaves kill each other in arenas and they wrote about it. People were opposed to treating slaves with cruelty and they wrote about it. But the whole idea of one person being the owner of the other - this to my knowledge was never mentioned as problematic.<br /> <br /> EDIT: The whole discussion makes me now realise that I don't quite understand how exactly to imagine slavery in Exalted.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":13416,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13416-lanic","user":"Lanic","date":". 11-26-2019, 02:01 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13416-lanic","name":"Lanic","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13416_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"194"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-11-26T11:40:33-08:00","id":1351079,"title":"","content":"Hmmm... I think I will step out of this thread until I've reread the Realm's section on slavery, as I'm obviously basing my ideas on things that have been changed in 3rd edition.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STing Bronze Age Exalted","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","name":"The Wizard of Oz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2402_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-11-26T11:49:36-08:00","id":1351081,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Wizard of Oz</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1350831-dynast-abolitionist?p=1351079#post1351079\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Hmmm... I think I will step out of this thread until I've reread the Realm's section on slavery, as I'm obviously basing my ideas on things that have been changed in 3rd edition.</div> </div> </div> </div>Heh. I took a glance at it and I'm more confused to be honest (AFK, proper engagement later)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4086-croakamancer","name":"Croakamancer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4086_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"490"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-11-26T12:35:11-08:00","id":1351095,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1350831-dynast-abolitionist?p=1351055#post1351055\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Slavery will be common in Ragara mines.<br /> <br /> Slavery will be common in V'neef vineyards.<br /> <br /> Peleps probably makes use of them to harvest lumber.<br /> <br /> And just generally utilised in commercial enterprises of the Dynasty, which blanket the Blessed Isle, as well as maintenance for various public works.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm not sure how accurate this is? Haven't gone through the Realm in depth but just on the direct section about slavery as a state, it doesn't really say <i>what</i> they do, just going into the mechanics of how it works. These all seem like logical extemporizations of what slavery on the isle might mean...<br /> <br /> But walking back a bit. Does chattel slavery really make sense on the Isle anyway?<br /> <br /> I'm not asking for 'is this canon', more curious about the logical chain here. The Realm <i>has </i>a large serf class, who are taught as an article of faith that it's their religious duty to toil for their betters who are enlightened by virtue of their birth. Any foreign slaves taken must be shipped across the Inland Sea, something that's pretty expensive, and require effort to break, house and keep in line. What advantage does the Dynasty get for using slave labour for manual work on its own doorstep like this? They can work them harder, or in more hazardous conditions, but those don't really seem to come up much on the isle. What risk is worth importing a slave over? <br /> <br /> I'm more willing to buy Realm slave work operations in the Threshold, where they've less far to ship and just as much stuff to mine/extract. And domestic slavedom on the isle makes sense, trophies/other individuals kept in bondage by the dynasty. But 'mass work slaves on the Isle' the more I think about it feels kinda off. Like it's there more for flavour than logic.<br /> <br /> (Which throws another wrinkle into abolitionist sentiment. Most of the people you're trying to help are thousands of miles away.)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4086-croakamancer","name":"Croakamancer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4086_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"490"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-11-26T13:52:43-08:00","id":1351113,"title":"","content":"They mentioned salt mining in 2nd ed, which (depending on the type) can be a pretty brutal and unpleasant job. Maybe the sort of thing peasants don't want to do.<br /> And there's prostitution of course.<br /> <br /> Vineyards seem like something peasants could do though.<br /> <br /> Now, if there are lots of slaves on the isle doing jobs that peasants could do, this could lead to another reason for a Realm Abolitionist. All these (heathen) slaves are taking jobs from honest Realm peasants.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STing Bronze Age Exalted","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","name":"The Wizard of Oz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2402_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-11-26T16:26:06-08:00","id":1351154,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>JohnDoe244</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1350831-dynast-abolitionist?p=1351069#post1351069\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Far more shockingly, citizens of the Realm may now also be enslaved.</div> </div> </div> </div>Although that's still supposed to be relatively uncommon, or at least a minority of the source of slaves.<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Wizard of Oz</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1350831-dynast-abolitionist?p=1351079#post1351079\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Hmmm... I think I will step out of this thread until I've reread the Realm's section on slavery, as I'm obviously basing my ideas on things that have been changed in 3rd edition.</div> </div> </div> </div>Don't forget the economics for House Cynis in What Fire Has Wrought, which also describes the market for specialty slaves.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Croakamancer</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1350831-dynast-abolitionist?p=1351095#post1351095\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">What advantage does the Dynasty get for using slave labour for manual work on its own doorstep like this?</div> </div> </div> </div>I find that the Dynasty would be sold on the point that they can profit more from their personal holdings being worked by slave forces, and that the Empress was motivated to have then utilize such to keep the Great Houses from having too much independent power over the peasantry that could cause economic disruption any time they prioritized their own profits over sustainable agriculture to keep the populace fed.<br /> <br /> It also intertwines with the Realm's foreign policy. They engage in a form of war in which acquiring large numbers of people to enslave becomes continuous, and it can also function as an assertion of their dominance over the Threshold to be constantly drawing slaves from their people.<br /> <br /> I don't quite think Realm peasants fall under the usual definition of serfdom. Their travel is restricted, sure, but that's based on centrally defined administrative divisions, not being tied to their tenancies, another thing that makes the Throne a higher form of authority in their lives than the Great Houses. There's no reference to them being restricted in traveling within a prefecture; I've never seen any indication that people aren't free to leave their village and go work in another or seek a new living in a town.<br /> <br /> I again see this as motivated by restricting the power of the Houses, asking some other economic concerns.<br /> <br /> EDIT: In addition to all of that, general ideas about the origin of slavery in the Realm, the current book has continuous reminders that in addition to general systemic inertia, we've got a version of House Cynis that works very hard to prevent that kind of labor from being a thing that you don't pay House Cynis for.<br /> <br /> You're getting Eyes Wide Shut parties where the centerpiece is some bondage show in which the actors give lectures on how dividing the attentions of peasants between their own farms and things like demesne obligations or corvee labour on public infrastructure makes all of those things less productive.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":158,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","user":"Isator Levi","date":". 11-26-2019, 07:50 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-11-26T20:15:32-08:00","id":1351209,"title":"","content":"Looking into some of the history of abolitionism, I'm discovering that a particular element that gave it momentum in Europe was actual slaves proactively taking advantage of legalisms by which they could argue that their own slavery was not enforceable. The jurisdictions of such cases might have been limited, but they'd serve as a basis for wide movements.<br /> <br /> Obviously the Realm provides no such recourse, and very much encodes the function of slavery in its law. I'm not quite sure about the facts of Roman emancipation, but that would seem to have a thing they have in common; it seems that in Roman jurisprudence, they essentially codified a thing that was not only evident beyond memory in their own society, but in virtually all societies they were in proximity to.<br /> <br /> It seems as though abolition in the Realm would depend very much on the rise of a new power that wants to specifically break from the old one ideologically, ala Revolutionary France.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-11-27T07:55:39-08:00","id":1351286,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1350831-dynast-abolitionist?p=1351154#post1351154\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I find that the Dynasty would be sold on the point that they can profit more from their personal holdings being worked by slave forces, and that the Empress was motivated to have then utilize such to keep the Great Houses from having too much independent power over the peasantry that could cause economic disruption any time they prioritized their own profits over sustainable agriculture to keep the populace fed.<br /> <br /> It also intertwines with the Realm's foreign policy. They engage in a form of war in which acquiring large numbers of people to enslave becomes continuous, and it can also function as an assertion of their dominance over the Threshold to be constantly drawing slaves from their people.<br /> <br /> I don't quite think Realm peasants fall under the usual definition of serfdom. Their travel is restricted, sure, but that's based on centrally defined administrative divisions, not being tied to their tenancies, another thing that makes the Throne a higher form of authority in their lives than the Great Houses. There's no reference to them being restricted in traveling within a prefecture; I've never seen any indication that people aren't free to leave their village and go work in another or seek a new living in a town.<br /> <br /> I again see this as motivated by restricting the power of the Houses, asking some other economic concerns.</div> </div> </div> </div>&quot;Restricting the power of the Houses&quot; AKA the same reason given for any self destructive decision the Empress made. :P <br /> <br /> I just don't buy it. I don't buy that it's more profitable to import slave labour en masse like this. I don't buy that slave labour is so ubiquitous and needed that every single project on the isle needs Cynis' approval to go ahead (That's as dumb as the changes 3E makes to the Iselsi, but side issue.) There's nothing about the way the Realm wages war that requires large numbers of captives - if you take all the people out of the satrapy, whose left to work the fields/mines back there?<br /> <br /> I suspect your view <i>is </i>closer to canonicity for 3e, but I dislike the canon if so - I'm coming around more and more to Oz's view - widespread use of slaves should be mostly a thing in Cynis holdings, with most regular labour force stuff being peasantry. The Isle's big - they don't need the extra hands to build a road or put up a statue.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4086-croakamancer","name":"Croakamancer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4086_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"490"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-11-27T10:18:30-08:00","id":1351310,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Croakamancer</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1350831-dynast-abolitionist?p=1351286#post1351286\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> &quot;Restricting the power of the Houses&quot; AKA the same reason given for any self destructive decision the Empress made. :P</div> </div> </div> </div>{exerts great effort to desalinate response}<br /> <br /> This is a simplification.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Croakamancer</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">I just don't buy it.</div> </div> </div> </div>Well you shouldn't have pirated it.<br /> <br /> Look, first and foremost the Realm serves as a composite of numerous empires to serve a literary and gameplay function. Some of the justifications for how those elements exist alongside each other are given in text, some aren't too difficult to project onto it from a position of familiarity with various historical conceits, and just a little bit requires the same kind of suspension of disbelief that comes from seeing a backdrop as anything other than painted cardboard so that people can get on with things.<br /> <br /> It appears to me that a good deal of the ideas underlying the Realm are based on trying to navigate the matter of creating a relatively stable monarchy when up against the challenge of an aristocracy in possession of literal supernatural powers, in the interests both of making the period in which the primary player characters appear contrast with the familiar history in a way that generates some gravitas, and giving the players of those superhuman aristocrats something with a bit more deft to work with than having always been totally anarchic. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Croakamancer</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">I don't buy that it's more profitable to import slave labour en masse like this.</div> </div> </div> </div>There are a couple of things to unpack here. One is that Romans did it, and indeed went much too far in that direction in a manner contributing greatly to their own instability.<br /> <br /> The other is that it doesn't need to be 100% true, it just needs to be something that the non-perfectly spherical Scarlet Dynasty can be credibly portrayed as having bought into. Slavery in the American South was coming to a point of not quite being worth the costs of it, but you still had plenty of people who were literally willing to fight to the death both on the belief that it was, and having built up a whole surrounding ideology around the preservation of it and that being a crux of their identity. With the information about the Realm that we have, it would not be hard to imagine many Dynasts who are deeply invested in the matter of how the exclusivity of slavery and the justification of such for them is a key detail of their elevated position, that they will fight tooth and nail to protect.<br /> <br /> Last is to interrogate the reason for why you don't think it would be sufficiently profitable.<br /> <br /> A tangential point is that the kinds of alternatives you're suggesting are historically the kind of thing that weaken monarchies.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Croakamancer</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">I don't buy that slave labour is so ubiquitous and needed that every single project on the isle needs Cynis' approval to go ahead (That's as dumb as the changes 3E makes to the Iselsi, but side issue.)</div> </div> </div> </div>I am perplexed by every part of this. Sticking to the on-topic components, I find that you are vastly inflating the use of slavery presented in the books even beyond my assertions, on top of bringing a presumption that Cynis would particularly care what the slaves it sells are used for any more than any historical slave traders have been.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Croakamancer</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">There's nothing about the way the Realm wages war that requires large numbers of captives</div> </div> </div> </div>It is reminiscent, now more than ever, of the Roman models of warfare that are <b>the</b> reason their society became so saturated with slaves.<br /> <br /> Of course, it's an open and ultimately unanswerable question about whether any form of historical warfare that was perceived by its undertakers to require taking the captives and large sections of the populace as slave labour actually did require it, but it has been a persistent viewpoint. For the Realm, functioning in the setting as a kind of quintessential empire, to share those sentiments (whether they are rationally vigorous or not) both makes it resonant with historical example and a more vividly antagonistic force in the setting for players to grapple with.<br /> <br /> Or, if your argument is that the Realm form of warfare actually fails to be written as one that would continuously funnel slaves back in the manner of the Romans, that could do with some backing up.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Croakamancer</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">if you take all the people out of the satrapy, whose left to work the fields/mines back there?</div> </div> </div> </div>This is not a necessary extreme of a conquest or the putting down of a rebellion producing large numbers of captives that can be sold as slaves.<br /> <br /> This is in no way written as the universal outcome of a Realm conquest.<br /> <br /> In cases where it does happen, the answer to your question can easily be immigrants, slaves relocated from another part of the empire at somebody else's expense, and the remaining workers having an opportunity for their wages to go up.<br /> <br /> Or more likely, the backstory of the satrapy having compelling conflict in which fewer people were required to do more work for no greater pay, as that difference went off to the Realm.<br /> <br /> &quot;Oh, it's completely irrational for a work force to notably decrease but for the people above them to expect at least as much if not more productivity from those who remain.&quot; Yeah, tell that to the next American company that orders mass layoffs and then expects profits to keep going up.<br /> <br /> Oh, actually, I've realised another alternative wherein the reduction in the labour pool coincides with investments by the new House dedicated to massively transforming the economy towards a purpose that benefits their bottom line. A satrapy's farming population has been greatly decreased by warfare and slave taking, such that there aren't enough workers to till the soil? Why, do what England did when its population was fucking halved by the Black Plague: buy a bunch of sheep, an animal that mostly takes care of itself in large groups that can be effectively watched by about one person, wait a couple of years for their population to greatly increase as they feed on former farmland that has been overrun with grass, and then sell the resulting textiles (and maybe even the meat) at a high markup in markets across the Blessed Isle and Threshold.<br /> <br /> What, the people of that satrapy have food shortages owing to the fact that most of the arable land has been converted into sheep pasture? Fortunately your homeland is the most productive source of staple grains in Creation, and between either producing it from your own slave plantations or buying it from the stockpilers or sharecroppers, you can then get enough to sell back to the satrapy at a premium. Or just pay the shepherds in it directly, it might be best in some cases to reduce their independent buying power.<br /> <br /> Probably none of these things are going to be uniformly applied across the Blessed Isle; its population is very big, its landscapes and economy very diverse, and the commercial interests of the Great Houses are numerous. There might be times when shortages in the slave supply or a section of House business lacking liquid capitol for a short-term expense of slaves mean that they hire labourers from among the peasants. Still, there are ways that serve the function in fiction to make sense of slave labour forces being generally preferred. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Croakmancer</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">widespread use of slaves should be mostly a thing in Cynis holdings</div> </div> </div> </div>I think the idea of only one House getting to make use of slave labour makes far less sense. How do the other Houses put up with that?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Croakamancer</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">The Isle's big - they don't need the extra hands to build a road or put up a statue.</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, the Isle being big kind of means that when you want to build a road, it will take the labour of lots of people.<br /> <br /> I find it's a question of what kind of labour people will be willing to volunteer for when there are more pleasant alternatives, how quickly you ever want any given work to get done, and how much you don't want to be seasonally dependent.<br /> <br /> The Blessed Isle probably has a population of about the same size as imperial China did at several points when it still needed to mandate corvee service whenever it wanted major public works done, and time spent having the people work on roads or canals was time not spent on managing the farms (also meaning that you had constraints against drawing on their labour at times when the harvest needed to be brought in), as well as being something that people were often uncooperative with (slaves come with the dubious benefit of being people you can dedicate time to conditioning against non-compliance). I find it credible that if they'd had the framework to draw on a continuous cycle of slave labour, Chinese authorities of those times would have preferred full time slave labourers and full time farmers to having farmers who need to dedicate part of their time to public service.<br /> <br /> I'm also thinking about how an element of medieval serfdom was that peasants needing to put in some of their time at the lord's demesne was something that even the lords found inconvenient in a manner that encouraged simply letting serfs pay cash in place of their obliged service, and paying for labourers. They wouldn't at the time have had access to large slave populations, and by the time they did the whole economic landscape (in addition to some ideological qualities) would preclude bringing slaves back to work land in Europe. But I think it points in a direction where aristocrats would have a preference for a more dedicated (and ultimately cheaper) labour force for their personal estates than the farmers being obliged to divide time between farms.<br /> <br /> Err, going back to the whole thing with ancient Italy, I just want to make sure that we're all on the same page that Roman slaves created massive amounts of unemployment among free citizens. Whatever a slave cost was not enough that Roman elites would go for employing the sizable population of that volcanic fertile peninsula for workers over just owning people. The Realm at least probably avoids the problem of unrest from massive unemployment by virtue of its scope and limitations on the scale of Dynast estates (if the Great Houses owned more land on the Isle, they would probably want slaves to work all of it and run up against there not being enough slaves in the world) in addition to strict limits on who can even buy slaves. But again, it's a historical example of slavery actively creating massive unemployment, rather than being some case of &quot;we need slaves because there aren't enough people to do the work&quot;. It's also the basis of my whole thing about how Dynasts might put profit over sustainable agriculture, because Roman aristocrats used their slaves to turn vast amounts of Italia into orchards; that's why they needed to import so much grain from Egypt.<br /> <br /> I've got to stop here, because I'm kind of falling into a death spiral of historical examples that back up the concept of the Scarlet Dynasty having a preference for slave labour that would create the setting as written. <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-11-27T14:37:22-08:00","id":1351361,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1350831-dynast-abolitionist?p=1351310#post1351310\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">{exerts great effort to desalinate response}<br /> <br /> This is a simplification.</div> </div> </div> </div>My reaction or your point? I'll admit my reaction is a simplification - the writers don't literally use that one crutch every time the Realm is given a weakness. But it comes up a lot when the time comes to discuss a more headbanging issue of how the Realm is run, and I'm just wondering how this collection of hotheads lasted seven <i>months </i>as a unified government, never mind seven hundred years. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/tongue.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Stick Out Tongue\" smilieid=\"5\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Well you shouldn't have pirated it. </div> </div> </div>Is this a joke? I don't get the humour in it. Just made me mildly more annoyed that you're accusing me of piracy.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Look, first and foremost the Realm serves as a composite of numerous empires to serve a literary and gameplay function. Some of the justifications for how those elements exist alongside each other are given in text, some aren't too difficult to project onto it from a position of familiarity with various historical conceits, and just a little bit requires the same kind of suspension of disbelief that comes from seeing a backdrop as anything other than painted cardboard so that people can get on with things.<br /> <br /> It appears to me that a good deal of the ideas underlying the Realm are based on trying to navigate the matter of creating a relatively stable monarchy when up against the challenge of an aristocracy in possession of literal supernatural powers, in the interests both of making the period in which the primary player characters appear contrast with the familiar history in a way that generates some gravitas, and giving the players of those superhuman aristocrats something with a bit more deft to work with than having always been totally anarchic. </div> </div> </div>That is the most... extensive way to phrase 'it's just a show, I should really just relax' I have ever seen. Puts me in mind of Cocteau's <i>Beauty and the Beast.</i><br /> <br /> Yes, the Realm is a fictional construct. But the detail I'm attacking here isn't a minor one. Work slavery on the Blessed Isle is a stupid construct inserted to add yet more cheap heat on the Realm. It's got precedence in the setting. I didn't even analyse it prior to this thread. But the more I think about it the more artifical and immersion breaking it becomes to me.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> There are a couple of things to unpack here. One is that Romans did it, and indeed went much too far in that direction in a manner contributing greatly to their own instability. </div> </div> </div>*shrug* And the Realm must do the thing the Romans did because?<br /> <br /> The Romans had a far shorter distance to ship their slaves than the Inland Sea. I can't find a good source on how long it takes to navigate the ocean, this is one of those points canon can end up forgetting, but the ol' <a href=\"https://whitewolf.fandom.com/wiki/Blessed_Isle\" target=\"_blank\">WW fandom wiki</a> suggested a voyage from Threshold to Isle would take multiple weeks, while the Romans could get a ship from Ostia to Alexandria in 6-8 days. Feeding a whole bunch of people, even feeding them slops across multiple weeks is a big undertaking. The cost benefit analysis for large scale slave shipments goes down in consequence.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The other is that it doesn't need to be 100% true, it just needs to be something that the non-perfectly spherical Scarlet Dynasty can be credibly portrayed as having bought into. Slavery in the American South was coming to a point of not quite being worth the costs of it, but you still had plenty of people who were literally willing to fight to the death both on the belief that it was, and having built up a whole surrounding ideology around the preservation of it and that being a crux of their identity. With the information about the Realm that we have, it would not be hard to imagine many Dynasts who are deeply invested in the matter of how the exclusivity of slavery and the justification of such for them is a key detail of their elevated position, that they will fight tooth and nail to protect. </div> </div> </div>Yeah, if you want to use the Dynasty as an analogy for the American South, go ahead.<br /> <br /> That's not a reason keeping it's beneficial, it's a justification for ignoring the logistical issues in the service of a potential good, that having slavery as a mass adopted and widely used thing within the Isle adds to the setting,,, somehow. I disagree. The logical leap doesn't get us anything - the Dynasty is still exploiting those at the bottom, there are still slaves on the isle, the humble folk are worked to the bone and told that's their divine duty. You've got all the same work slavery going on off the isle - mining and producing and funnelling goods back to the Dynasty. You've got plenty of material for showing the evils of the Realm.<br /> <br /> You don't need to add something that's a logical leap.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Last is to interrogate the reason for why you don't think it would be sufficiently profitable.<br /> <br /> A tangential point is that the kinds of alternatives you're suggesting are historically the kind of thing that weaken monarchies. </div> </div> </div>Primarily, as aforementioned, the inland sea. Furthermore I don't think any historical monarchy has quite as strong a religious hand as the Immaculate Order and Dynastic rule provides - the guys at the top are literally magic and the religion is based almost entirely around conformity and social unity. Actually having the workers work isn't gonna weaken Dragon Blooded rule one bit.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I am perplexed by every part of this. Sticking to the on-topic components, I find that you are vastly inflating the use of slavery presented in the books even beyond my assertions, on top of bringing a presumption that Cynis would particularly care what the slaves it sells are used for any more than any historical slave traders have been. </div> </div> </div>Your assertion was that slaves would be everywhere. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/tongue.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Stick Out Tongue\" smilieid=\"5\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> That they'd be used for manual labour jobs across the isle, that there's nowhere you could go without running into slave labor. I don't think the books back that up, but I feel I categorized your point accurately. If you disagree, fair enough: I'm happy to hear a clarification.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> It is reminiscent, now more than ever, of the Roman models of warfare that are <b>the</b> reason their society became so saturated with slaves.<br /> <br /> Of course, it's an open and ultimately unanswerable question about whether any form of historical warfare that was perceived by its undertakers to require taking the captives and large sections of the populace as slave labour actually did require it, but it has been a persistent viewpoint. For the Realm, functioning in the setting as a kind of quintessential empire, to share those sentiments (whether they are rationally vigorous or not) both makes it resonant with historical example and a more vividly antagonistic force in the setting for players to grapple with. </div> </div> </div>.<br /> A more 'vividly antagonistic' force. Which is what I've been thinking from the start - it's primarily an attempt to dirty up their image and remind players that they're the bad guys than something that flows logically. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> <br /> <br /> Remember, my objection was not to the Realm taking slaves. It was focused on mass work slave crews being transported to and used on the Blessed Isle, since the logistics seemed off. IRL, when you moved a ton of slaves a long way like that you had something specific in mind you needed them to do, right? The Triangle Trade didn't arise out of nowhere, it was to fulfil a demand. The Isle doesn't have that. Generic undercutting of the labour market is an arguable reason, but that's a hell of a lot easier to pull off for the Romans than for the Realm. Especially when they've got a strong alternative<br /> <br /> Operations on the Threshold.<br /> <br /> Seriously, why ship people all that way to chop wood or mine stone when you can just have them do that in the Threshold, and ship back the goods they make? Threshold kingdoms also traffic in slaves quite a bit. Hell, that's basically how the Lap operates, and how Deshan worked back in 2e. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I think the idea of only one House getting to make use of slave labour makes far less sense. How do the other Houses put up with that? </div> </div> </div>Really really easily if slavery on the isle is a limited institution anyway due to the logistics and thus doesn't come up every day. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I find it's a question of what kind of labour people will be willing to volunteer for when there are more pleasant alternatives, how quickly you ever want any given work to get done, and how much you don't want to be seasonally dependent. </div> </div> </div>Isn't the idea that they get a choice oddly modern? When you can't read and don't have much in the way of funds, what's your alternative to working?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I'm also thinking about how an element of medieval serfdom was that peasants needing to put in some of their time at the lord's demesne was something that even the lords found inconvenient in a manner that encouraged simply letting serfs pay cash in place of their obliged service, and paying for labourers. They wouldn't at the time have had access to large slave populations, and by the time they did the whole economic landscape (in addition to some ideological qualities) would preclude bringing slaves back to work land in Europe. But I think it points in a direction where aristocrats would have a preference for a more dedicated (and ultimately cheaper) labour force for their personal estates than the farmers being obliged to divide time between farms. </div> </div> </div>I'm fairly sure the same ideological preferences should kick in for the Dynasty. 3e tries hard to water it down, but the principle that Dynasts are the stewards of their people is still in there - making large swathes of the populous unemployed and starving doesn't mesh well with that. (Plus as above, the foundation of my dispute is the infrastructure to make mass enslavement on the Isle isn't really there.)<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Err, going back to the whole thing with ancient Italy, I just want to make sure that we're all on the same page that Roman slaves created massive amounts of unemployment among free citizens. Whatever a slave cost was not enough that Roman elites would go for employing the sizable population of that volcanic fertile peninsula for workers over just owning people. The Realm at least probably avoids the problem of unrest from massive unemployment by virtue of its scope and limitations on the scale of Dynast estates (if the Great Houses owned more land on the Isle, they would probably want slaves to work all of it and run up against there not being enough slaves in the world) in addition to strict limits on who can even buy slaves. But again, it's a historical example of slavery actively creating massive unemployment, rather than being some case of &quot;we need slaves because there aren't enough people to do the work&quot;. It's also the basis of my whole thing about how Dynasts might put profit over sustainable agriculture, because Roman aristocrats used their slaves to turn vast amounts of Italia into orchards; that's why they needed to import so much grain from Egypt.<br /> <br /> I've got to stop here, because I'm kind of falling into a death spiral of historical examples that back up the concept of the Scarlet Dynasty having a preference for slave labour that would create the setting as written. </div> </div> </div>We're on the same page. I just think we want different things. You're okay stretching logic a bit because you feel it fits in spirit, that the logistical issues are minor, and you like the result. I feel the logical issues are major and the result is essentially unchanged, Plus as a rule I like to try and keep the Realm ambiguous and somewhat lowkey defensible rather than 'vividly antagonistic'. But that feels like a side issue here. Both outcomes have slavery, have work gangs too. Neither leaves the Dynasty on a different moral ground. The only issue is that having isle labour be slave driven seems silly to me when the peasantry exists right there.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4086-croakamancer","name":"Croakamancer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4086_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"490"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-11-29T03:00:37-08:00","id":1351621,"title":"","content":"I keep thinking about ways in which slavery in the Realm relates to farming, particularly concerning the matter of how harvesting is going to be its most labor intensive period.<br /> <br /> There are surely at least parts of the Realm in which the peasants who spend the year growing the crop are able to hire on extra labour from other peasants of less consistent employment, or even on the cheap when there are dispossessed in the area.<br /> <br /> But I would not find it inconceivable that there are areas with labour shortages for one reason or another, in which the farmers need to pay a Great House to direct some of their slaves to the task of bringing in the harvest.<br /> <br /> It feels a little off to the power dynamic for them to provide service to people of a lower class, but I imagine there are ways the society contrives it along the lines of paying tribute for the privilege of access to an exclusive resource. In a similar manner to how Medieval European peasants needed to pay to access manor monopolies on mills or bread ovens.<br /> <br /> After all, it has to be the kind of perspective that already reconciles Mnemon's high status with working as architects and in construction. That's work that has definitely had prestige, but is still typically servile.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-11-29T12:29:37-08:00","id":1351701,"title":"","content":"I would tend to have an intuition on abolitionism, in a strict sense, and as a movement, as unlikely in the Realm, as this would require staking out a moral absolutist position that is at odds with how the Realm's religion works. As a structurally hierarchical form without radical commitments to too many further unambiguous, moral imperatives and religious laws (as opposed rather to interpretable aphorism and metaphor) which would constrict Dragonblooded and Sidereal freedom to act in whatever means they deem justify the ends. <br /> <br /> Abolitionism in our world came from a combination of religious imperatives to treat all co-believers in egalitarian &quot;brotherhood&quot; and civic imperatives under the influence of popular democracy to establish universal rights of a citizen and human being within a territory. This is really happening most recently in the 18th century and overrunning into the 19th, but more generally in particular sorts of societies at particular sorts of times. Without this, slavery can become distasteful and economically impractical and so marginal (in New World slavery, there are frequent examples where slavery was about to or did become a marginal mode of labour). But it is hard to move from this to legal commitments that is should never happen, rather than commitments which moderate the practice if they speak on it at all (although usually only as social convention!). (Even in these religions that stress a brotherhood of the faith, these can even include measures of accommodation in religious law and orthodoxy which practically would prevent a rise of much of a belief in abolition, as in Islam).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/929-ghosthead","name":"Ghosthead","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2113"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-11-29T16:39:28-08:00","id":1351741,"title":"","content":"I generally agree with you, but I could actually see the religious mandate of the Realm pushing some (not all) believes to oppose slavery, because they believe that slavers are not doing what they're supposed to; ie converting them.<br /> <br /> I'm thinking of some of the stuff that happened with native American slaves in Spanish and Portugese colonies. The justification was that it was okay to enslave locals in order to teach them Christianity. But a lot of slavers weren't that fussed about teaching their slaves Christianity, partly because they, well, just didn't care, and couldn't be bothered, but also because that might mean in the future that their slaves were Christians and then they'd have to treat them better, or even let them go. Sometimes they even made it harder for their slaves to learn about Christianity.<br /> <br /> Then you had things like how slavers would turn up at a village to enslave everyone, and the locals would say &quot;Gloria Dei! Maria! Jesus!&quot; and do the sign of the cross (because they'd learnt from other indigenous people that this was the way to not get enslaved). And sometimes it'd work, and sometimes slavers would just enslave them anyway, and pretend they didn't see any Christian stuff, which would really piss off the Jesuit who'd been through their last week and believed (truly or not) he'd converted the locals.<br /> <br /> So, in the end, the Pope just decided the whole thing was a mess, and that it was actually getting in the way of converting America, so he declared that all slavery of the indigenous people of North+South America was forbidden. (So they enslaved Africans instead. There were other reasons, some more important, but that was one.)<br /> <br /> Anyway, I can imagine some Monks deciding that Cynis has well and truly failed in their attempts to convert the slaves to the Immaculate Order, that slaving had interfered with missionaries' attempts to convert the Threshold (as the barbarians keep insisting that the Dynasty who kidnapped their relatives are not, in fact, the good guys), that slavery is out of place in the Perfected Hierarchy, and so the whole thing should be scrapped.<br /> Not that I expect this to be a common view, but it could definitely be the view of some people.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STing Bronze Age Exalted","edited":{"userid":2402,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","user":"The Wizard of Oz","date":". 11-29-2019, 07:42 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","name":"The Wizard of Oz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2402_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-11-29T18:02:06-08:00","id":1351749,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Wizard of Oz</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1350831-dynast-abolitionist?p=1351741#post1351741\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Not that I expect this to be a common view, but it could definitely be the view of some people.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's probably the best way to do it anyway. You don't want there to exist absolutely none at all, which might make people just think it's flat out impossible and don't try, but if every other character is petitioning for it in the senate then your character seems a bit pointless, as it's just about to happen anyway.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1121-drlovemonkey","name":"DrLoveMonkey","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1121_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4736"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-11-29T20:46:35-08:00","id":1351782,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lanic</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1350831-dynast-abolitionist?p=1351000#post1351000\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">As far as I know, in the whole vast ethical literature of antiquity there is no single person to question the institution of slavery.</div> </div> </div> </div>While we only have fragments of Alcidamas's <i>Messeniakos</i>, the bits we have seem to be pretty anti-slavery. And the fact that it was found necessary to justify slavery (like Aristotle's claims about the existence of slaves by nature, in direct contrast to Alcidamas's claim that nature has made no man a slave) itself implies there were challenges to it. And as the survival of writings from antiquity was not a result of an unbiased process, a lack of any given argument in the works we have might simply indicate that people in a position to preserve works didn't favor those arguments.<br /> <br /> And in China, slavery was actually abolished by Wang Mang (though, obviously, it didn't stick; he reversed himself).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":572,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/572-verzio","user":"Verzio","date":". 11-29-2019, 11:52 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/572-verzio","name":"Verzio","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar572_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"380"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-11-30T03:16:50-08:00","id":1351823,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Verzio</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1350831-dynast-abolitionist?p=1351782#post1351782\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">While we only have fragments of Alcidamas's <i>Messeniakos</i>, the bits we have seem to be pretty anti-slavery. And the fact that it was found necessary to justify slavery (like Aristotle's claims about the existence of slaves by nature, in direct contrast to Alcidamas's claim that nature has made no man a slave) itself implies there were challenges to it.</div> </div> </div> </div>Thank you for the reference, I will check it. However, taking into account the extremely systematic treatment that Aristotle was giving to pretty much everything, the fact that he discusses some issue doesn't necessarily mean that it didn't have the status of the received obvious view. It was not really challenged in the same way as XIXth century US abolitionists challenged it. But as I said, I will take a look at Messaniakos' fragments.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Verzio</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1350831-dynast-abolitionist?p=1351782#post1351782\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">And as the survival of writings from antiquity was not a result of an unbiased process, a lack of any given argument in the works we have might simply indicate that people in a position to preserve works didn't favor those arguments.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think that this is not really a good point. Much of the traditional roman literature comes from senator upper class, so this is clearly biased. However, in the writings of these historians we do not really see anything like abolitionism. We see slaves' uprisings, we see Christians, we see ecstatic cult from the east, this all through upper-class Roman lens, but we do not really see abolitionists in the sense discussed here. They were clearly biased in their presentation, but it definitely wasn't a common practice to just ignore important issues, the way XXth century propaganda did it. Instead, we see Marcus Aurelius, an emperor-philosopher who decided to choose ascetic live despite enormous wealth and heavily tried to replace gladiator fights with non-lethal sports out of ethical concern, but without a slight hint of abolitionism in his writings.<br /> <br /> However, the major bias in survival of ancient literature is that it was more likely for christian literature to be copied and this gives us the other end of the spectrum. Christianity was treated with contempt in most preserved Roman sources, int. al. as &quot;religion of slaves and women.&quot; Christian literature brought to us some writings of people from lower classes, writings giving a glimpse into their lives, or a prison diary of an upper-middle-class woman Perpetua. In this literature, we can read much about slavery, but we still do not find slavery condemned as an institution. We can read that masters should be good to their slaves, that they shouldn't be cruel, that they shouldn't be unjust, that definitely one shouldn't watch gladiator's fights, that slaves are definitely people, but we also read that the fact that a slave and master both became Christians doesn't mean that the said slave shouldn't stay obedient to the master. Really, they lived in a completely different mindset.<br /> <br /> My point was mostly this: when reading about abolitionism in the Realm in this and other threads, I think that most people depicted abolitionism in such a distant culture as an obvious stance that any sane person would jump into. And yet, we can see that a whole millennia-spanning civilisation can never really think about it more deeply.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":13416,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13416-lanic","user":"Lanic","date":". 11-30-2019, 06:21 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13416-lanic","name":"Lanic","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13416_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"194"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-11-30T03:41:57-08:00","id":1351833,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar2402_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"2402\" data-vbnamecard=\"2402\">The Wizard of Oz</a>, that's an interesting qualification and probably correct.<br /> <br /> On a bit of a tangent to the issue of slavery in the Realm I'm now thinking afresh about how radical it was and a statement of intent in tone and theme for Exalted to introduce the Immaculate Philosophy in its first book.<br /> <br /> &quot;Here's a game that's all about the being the Chosen of the Gods. (And now, most other, popular fantasy games to this point tend to present this idea through Paladins and Clerics).<br /> <br /> And now we will immediately introduce ideas deconstructing the moral superiority of the Chosen of the Gods by introducing an evocative evil-anime church that is based an exactly artificially constructed abuse of the idea that being the Chosen of the Gods makes one morally superior and unquestionable. Oh and we'll tie it to Shaolin Monks (which hits another note in our influences, by the way). <br /> <br /> But as we're *good* writers and not just unremitting edgy cynics, we'll also show how goodness, compassion, humanity and a longing for a higher spiritual truth survives even this structure by introducing characters and history that demonstrates that, and who can subvert much of the intent of these oppressive structures in ways unexpected to their architects&quot;.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/929-ghosthead","name":"Ghosthead","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2113"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-11-30T03:52:44-08:00","id":1351834,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ghosthead</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1350831-dynast-abolitionist?p=1351833#post1351833\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar2402_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"2402\" data-vbnamecard=\"2402\">The Wizard of Oz</a>, that's an interesting qualification and probably correct.<br /> <br /> On a bit of a tangent to the issue of slavery in the Realm I'm now thinking afresh about how radical it was and a statement of intent in tone and theme for Exalted to introduce the Immaculate Philosophy in its first book.<br /> <br /> &quot;Here's a game that's all about the being the Chosen of the Gods. (And now, most other, popular fantasy games to this point tend to present this idea through Paladins and Clerics).<br /> <br /> And now we will immediately introduce ideas deconstructing the moral superiority of the Chosen of the Gods by introducing an evocative evil-anime church that is based an exactly artificially constructed abuse of the idea that being the Chosen of the Gods makes one morally superior and unquestionable. Oh and we'll tie it to Shaolin Monks (which hits another note in our influences, by the way).[...]&quot;</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> That's very interesting. Where does this quote come from? It's interesting how presentation changed, because from reading Exalted 3 ed core book or What Fire Has Wrought, I really just got the impression that the Realm is actually quite standard empire, with quite standard imperialist practices and quite standard imperialism-justifying-ideology. Definitely not &quot;evil-anime&quot;. Maybe I simply tend to set a low bar.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13416-lanic","name":"Lanic","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13416_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"194"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-11-30T03:58:09-08:00","id":1351836,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13416-lanic\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar13416_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"13416\" data-vbnamecard=\"13416\">Lanic</a>, unfortunately I've misled you with a paraphrase of what am I inferring they tried to do and put it in quotes mark, not actually quoting anyone <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> Edit: To add, I do think you're correct if you're also saying that, in a more general sense less about the Immaculate Philosophy specifically (or the Evil Anime Church trope), that the Realm is meant to be a more pragmatic treatment of empire than in purely sensationalizing pulp fantasy and D&amp;D offshoots thereof. The intent absolutely is offering a perspective that hews close to real huge central empires like Rome and China, and where the Realm's brand of authoritarian injustice derives from logical extrapolation, with verisimilitude, of its immortal sorcerer-empress, its power flowing through family lines and a critique of the often internally dysfunctional tools authoritarian leaders use to keep themselves in power against challengers from their elites, and not simply being an arbitrary evilness that the audience can't take seriously.<br /> <br /> But I think it still does lean into tropes of the evil central empire that a lot of its sources tend to use - as almost invariably present in a huge % of Moorcock's pulp fantasy, essentially every Final Fantasy and even JRPG game in the 90s, many anime like Escaflowne or Nausicaa - rather than offering a purely realistic form, and does so in a way that is somewhat critical of imperialism, which seems about right (as much as I think there are bad ways of doing so that spread false ideas about modern-day real world development gaps being based on the fruits of resources and labour extracted through imperialism, or that recent imperialism in 1500-1900 tended to disrupt some sort of world of egalitarian communities living in peace and high levels of prosperity. But that's a tangent.).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":929,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/929-ghosthead","user":"Ghosthead","date":". 11-30-2019, 07:28 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/929-ghosthead","name":"Ghosthead","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2113"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-11-30T10:30:58-08:00","id":1351882,"title":"","content":"EDIT: Noo, changed my mind. Now's not the time.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":158,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","user":"Isator Levi","date":". 11-30-2019, 02:03 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-11-30T12:05:06-08:00","id":1351894,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Croakamancer</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1350831-dynast-abolitionist?p=1351361#post1351361\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">your point?</div> </div> </div> </div>I think I still ultimately disagree, and that there are still some counters for some of your points, but I find from a rudimentary look at your post that I can manage from my phone and current state of mind that you're illustrating a fair number of flaws in my reasoning and weaknesses in my argument method and purpose. I intend at some point to give the post a proper read and full consideration, and reassess some of my own ideas accordingly. <br /> <br /> I'm not sure if I'll make a response to it, but if I do I'll really hope to pursue it in terms of two-way discussion to recognise and respect differing viewpoints, rather than gratifying my ego.<br /> <br /> There are a couple of things where I think my meaning has been misread, but I find them to be in a lack of clarity or consistency in a few spots for my part.<br /> <br /> For the time being, sticking to the essential topic, I've started wondering at the idea that the Realm could be portrayed as having improved the attraction of slavery by offering things like subsidies or tax breaks to Dynast operations that utilize their labour. Something that could be motivated by what the slave trade contributes to their foreign policy, helping to keep House Cynis prominent and the awkward question of what's to be done with large numbers of slaves of a Great House no longer wants them.<br /> <br /> In a case like that, a motive in abolition of slavery could be in people who see more beneficial economics in its place, and a method could be in removing any such financial props.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}